<Project>

  <!--
    Shared, lock-step package-version baseline. Every Bodu library ships at BoduBaseVersion by
    default, so consumers can treat a matched version number across all Bodu.* packages as a
    coherent set.

    Breaking rank: a single library that needs an out-of-band fix overrides this deliberately by
    setting <BoduPackageVersionOverride> in its own .csproj. That property is applied (and reported)
    in the repository-root Directory.Build.targets, which is processed after the project body so the
    divergence is explicit, visible in build output, and auditable in source control. Do not edit
    <VersionPrefix> directly in a project to break rank - use BoduPackageVersionOverride so the
    intent is obvious.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BoduBaseVersion>1.3.0</BoduBaseVersion>
    <VersionPrefix>$(BoduBaseVersion)</VersionPrefix>

    <!--
      The second version stream. Since the 1.0.0 cut the solution ships two lock-step streams, split
      along the API-stability tier each package's README declares:

        Stable                  ships at BoduBaseVersion    (the default; no per-project setting)
        Preview / Experimental  ships at BoduPreviewVersion (via BoduPackageVersionOverride below)

      The split exists so the 1.0 compatibility promise is made only by packages that can keep it.
      A preview package stays below 1.0 until its API settles, at which point promoting it is two
      edits: retier its README banner and delete its override. Until its first Stable release is on
      nuget.org it also pins PackageValidationBaselineVersion, in its csproj, to its last preview
      release: it has never shipped at the Stable baseline below, so a pack compared with that
      baseline would fail with NU1102.

      A preview package references this property rather than pinning a literal, so moving the
      preview stream stays a one-line edit here. bld/check-release-manifest.sh enforces both that and
      the tier-to-stream agreement, because nothing else reconciles a package's declared tier with
      the version it actually publishes at - and a wrong version cannot be taken back off nuget.org.
    -->
    <BoduPreviewVersion>0.7.1</BoduPreviewVersion>

    <!--
      Package-validation baselines, one per version stream. Each is its stream's last lock-step
      release, so a pack runs the ApiCompat comparison against the release that stream's consumers
      have now. The streams publish different versions, and a pack compared with a version its
      package never shipped at fails with NU1102 before comparing any API, so neither value can
      serve both: BoduPackageValidationBaseline applies to the Stable packages, and
      BoduPreviewPackageValidationBaseline to the packages that ship at BoduPreviewVersion.

      After a release, move the baseline of each stream it published (bld/RELEASING.md,
      Post-publish): the Stable one after every lock-step release, the preview one only after a
      release that moved BoduPreviewVersion. bld/check-release-manifest.sh fails a baseline ahead of
      its stream's version, and a preview stream without a baseline of its own.

      Empty = validation stays inert for that stream (no published baseline exists yet). A baseline
      is applied only to packages listed in bld/release-manifest.txt, once it has reached the version
      each first shipped at - packages that have never shipped have nothing to compare against - and
      only to a build with BoduSignAssembly=true (the default), because the published packages are
      strong-named and a comparison against them needs the same assembly identity. Both are applied
      in the repository-root Directory.Build.targets.
    -->
    <BoduPackageValidationBaseline>1.0.0</BoduPackageValidationBaseline>
    <BoduPreviewPackageValidationBaseline>0.7.1</BoduPreviewPackageValidationBaseline>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
